WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Grant from Iowa City.&nbsp;
Grant, welcome to the show. What's going on?&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.980
Speaker 4: Hey, David. So I am about&nbsp;
a decade late in asking this question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.320
but I hadn't really paid attention to politics up&nbsp;
until the last couple of years ago. Okay. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.660
I was thinking back to Obama's&nbsp;
presidency and how there was such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.640
drama surrounding his birth certificate&nbsp;
and all that, of course. And I was curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.660
how what's your perspective on requirement&nbsp;
for people to be natural born citizens in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.500
order to be eligible for president?
Speaker 1: Yeah, I watched this a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.260
little bit before. I mean, listen,&nbsp;
I. I am more in the direction of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:52.740
Naturalized citizens should be eligible,&nbsp;
primarily for the reason that without that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:59.160
you really are creating sort of two tiers of&nbsp;
citizens. You've got everybody is a citizen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:07.440
on paper, but some are kind of like more citizens&nbsp;
than others, if that makes sense. Now, I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.380
that it would be reasonable to say naturalized&nbsp;
citizens can be president, but you have to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.420
some additional you know, you've lived in the&nbsp;
U.S. for X amount of years. I'm completely open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.780
to something like that. But I mean, listen,&nbsp;
not that I have any interest in running for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.380
president. I was five when I moved to the United&nbsp;
States. I've been here more than 30 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.520
Does it really make sense for me not to be allowed&nbsp;
to be president on that basis? I mean, I grew up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:39.660
here. I've only voted here. I've only worked here.&nbsp;
I don't know. It just it doesn't really seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:46.260
that's that that's problematic. But I am fine&nbsp;
with there being some requirement where, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.420
you if you've only been here, if you only been a&nbsp;
citizen for a year, maybe you can't be president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.640
But I'll have to tell you. I also understand the&nbsp;
argument of just letting the people decide. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.980
voters say, hey, you know what? Even though this&nbsp;
person's a citizen, they've only been a citizen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:06.180
a year. I don't think that that's who we need as&nbsp;
president. You could also make the case that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.500
don't even really need a rule. It should just&nbsp;
be up to the voters to decide. So yeah, for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.320
I am significantly in the direction of allowing&nbsp;
naturalized citizens to run for president.&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.940
Speaker 4: Right. That makes sense to me too,&nbsp;
because I can completely understand. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.440
the current requirement is you have to live in&nbsp;
the US for 14 years or be a citizen for 14 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:33.120
and you have to be 35 and like that makes sense to&nbsp;
me. The 35 years old is an arbitrary number, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.260
I guess it's understandable what you want people&nbsp;
to have a life experience that makes sense to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:44.400
But yeah, the idea that you have to be a natural&nbsp;
born citizen is some people who literally can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:51.660
find an option for people to have like like for&nbsp;
yourself. And you do not plan to run. But like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.840
everyone has the least possibility of living in&nbsp;
the U.S. for 15 years. If you have the funds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.440
make it here and, you know, make the move. Yep.
Speaker 1: But you can't go back in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.509
and be born somewhere else, that's for sure.
Speaker 4: Right, exactly. Yeah. So. All right.&nbsp;

00:03:05.509 --> 00:03:07.020
Speaker 1: Grant. Well, thank&nbsp;
you. I appreciate the question.&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.500
Speaker 4: I ask one extra little thing here.
Speaker 1: Oh, sure.&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:17.400
Speaker 4: Do you see there being a possibility of&nbsp;
that requirement being changed in the future? Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.920
that something that could be twisters up to? Sure.
Speaker 1: Yeah. I mean, I see a possibility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.820
it, although it doesn't seem like there's really&nbsp;
any big movement that's getting attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.740
pushing for it right now.&nbsp;
So I wouldn't expect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.460
to happen any time soon.
Speaker 4: Okay. Again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.100
All right. Well, thanks for taking my call.
Speaker 1: All right. Thanks, Grant. There's Grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:40.500
from Iowa City with a very important question,&nbsp;
which everyone will say, my opinion doesn't matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.020
because I'm biased or something like that. Well,&nbsp;
then listen to someone else's opinion. Let's go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.020
Smitty from Los Angeles. Smitty, what's going on?
Speaker 5: Good morning, David. How are you? I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:04:00.240
doing well. Sorry, not cut me off. I'm sorry.&nbsp;
I mean, it's habit. Well, I just wanted to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:07.260
after, as I've continued to listen to you, I&nbsp;
have discovered that pretty much my politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.900
are exactly where yours is. Wow. I mean, I.&nbsp;
I definitely feel like, you know, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:19.620
certain things that. You know that we need to&nbsp;
take a hold of from a public perspective with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:28.380
education, with environment, with, you know,&nbsp;
certain industries that we should really have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:35.040
control over from the government perspective with&nbsp;
a lot of accountability. I think one of the issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:43.440
is also accountability. And this is my question.&nbsp;
Do you think it really is so much about left and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.640
right, or do you think it really is more about&nbsp;
accountability? And do you think that it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:57.720
is a matter of how you structure your society, but&nbsp;
really having that accountability behind it? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.640
